# go-poker configuration. Copy to .env and edit.
#
# After changing anything here, recreate the containers:
#   docker compose up -d --force-recreate
# A plain `restart` is not enough for values the Go server reads at boot, and
# while the app is running the mounted file can go stale: a bind-mounted FILE
# is pinned to the inode it had at container start, and editors save by
# renaming, so the container ends up reading a deleted inode ("I/O error") and
# never sees the new content. The settings the app must have (REDIS_URL,
# LIVEKIT_*) are therefore also passed as real environment variables by the
# compose files, which are resolved from this file at container creation.

# Redis password. The compose entrypoint starts redis with
# --requirepass "$REDIS_PASSWORD" (wrapped in a shell because the redis
# image does not expand env vars itself).
REDIS_PASSWORD="password"
# REDIS_URL must carry the same credentials. Redis 6+ ACL syntax: username
# "default", then the password. Keep the password in sync with REDIS_PASSWORD
# above (nested $VAR expansion in .env is not reliable across compose
# versions, so the value is written out explicitly).
REDIS_URL="redis://default:password@redis:6379"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTPS (optional). Leave everything unset to serve plain HTTP on PORT (8080),
# e.g. behind a reverse proxy / CDN that terminates TLS for you.
#
# Option A — automatic Let's Encrypt certificate (ACME). Needs the host to be
# reachable from the internet on ports 80 and 443 with DNS pointing at it.
# The certificate is cached in TLS_CACHE_DIR (a docker volume in compose) and
# renewed automatically.
#TLS_DOMAINS="poker.example.com"
#TLS_EMAIL="you@example.com"
#TLS_CACHE_DIR="/app/certs"
#
# Option B — your own certificate (bought, certbot, or self-signed for a LAN).
# Mount the files into the container and point at them.
# Note: browsers expose the microphone only on https or localhost, so voice
# chat from phones on the LAN (http://192.168.x.x:8080) is listen-only until
# the site is served over https. A self-signed cert works once the phone
# trusts it, e.g.:
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 3650 \
#     -keyout certs/privkey.pem -out certs/fullchain.pem \
#     -subj "/CN=192.168.0.104" -addext "subjectAltName=IP:192.168.0.104"
#   then install certs/fullchain.pem on the phone (iOS: AirDrop/Mail the file,
#   Settings > General > VPN & Device Management > install, then
#   Settings > General > About > Certificate Trust Settings > enable).
#TLS_CERT_FILE="/app/certs/fullchain.pem"
#TLS_KEY_FILE="/app/certs/privkey.pem"
#
# Port the HTTPS listener binds inside the container (compose maps it to
# HTTPS_PORT on the host as well). With TLS on, the plain PORT (8080, mapped
# to HTTP_PORT on the host) only serves ACME challenges and redirects to https.
#HTTPS_PORT=443
#HTTP_PORT=80

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Abuse protection (all optional; the defaults shown are applied when unset).
# Kernel-level SYN-flood protection is configured on the host, not here: see
# deploy/HARDENING.md.
#
# Take the client IP from X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP. ONLY enable behind a
# reverse proxy you control, otherwise clients can spoof their IP and dodge
# the per-IP limits.
#TRUST_PROXY=false
# HTTP requests per second per IP (burst = 2x). 429 when exceeded.
#RATE_HTTP_RPS=30
# WebSocket messages per second per connection (burst = 2x). The socket is
# closed when exceeded.
#RATE_WS_MSGS=20
# Login / register attempts per minute per IP (brute-force protection).
#RATE_AUTH_PER_MIN=10
# Concurrent WebSocket connections per IP and in total. Extra handshakes get
# a 429 before any upgrade work is done.
#MAX_CONNS_PER_IP=10
#MAX_CONNS=2000
# Maximum number of live rooms (a room is recycled 2 min after it empties).
#MAX_TABLES=200
# Comma-separated WebSocket Origin allow-list. Empty = accept any origin
# (needed for `make hot`, where the page is on :8080 and the socket on :3000).
# Set it in production to your site origin(s).
#ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://poker.example.com"

# Deep CFR inference server (deepcfr-texas-no-limit-holdem-6-players,
# server/inference_server.py), e.g. uvicorn on :8001. When set AND its
# /healthz probe answers, rooms may be created with botType "ai": their bots
# decide via the model (per-action fallback to the built-in heuristic).
# Rooms with the default botType "normal" never call the service. Unset =
# AI bots are not offered at all.
# NOTE for docker: the server runs inside the container, where 127.0.0.1 is
# the container itself. Point at a service on the host via
# host.docker.internal (the compose files map it with host-gateway on
# Linux), or run the inference server as a compose service on the same
# network and use its service name.
#AI_INFERENCE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8001

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-time knobs (read by docker compose, not by the running app).
#
# npm registry used when the frontend image is built. Unset = npm's default
# (registry.npmjs.org), which can be very slow from some networks — the whole
# ~510-package install took 67s against npmjs.org vs 9s against a mirror on
# the same machine. A mirror configured in your ~/.npmrc does NOT reach the
# build: only this setting does. Example:
#NPM_REGISTRY="https://registry.npmmirror.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Voice chat: LiveKit SFU (the OPT-IN `livekit` service in docker compose).
# In-room voice runs on LiveKit: every browser sends/receives audio through
# one self-hosted SFU instead of direct peer connections. The Go server only
# mints short-lived access tokens (get-livekit-token → livekit-token) that
# admit each player to the LiveKit room "gopoker-<tablename>"; all WebRTC
# signalling and NAT traversal live inside livekit-server.
#
# Start the livekit container alongside the stack (compose reads this file
# for COMPOSE_* variables too). Leave commented out to run without voice.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=voice
# The API key pair shared by livekit-server and the game server. LiveKit
# requires a secret of at least 32 chars: `openssl rand -hex 32`. Without
# both set the game server answers get-livekit-token with ok:false and the
# client shows "voice chat unavailable" instead of failing.
#LIVEKIT_API_KEY=gopoker
#LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
# Address of the LiveKit server AS REACHED FROM THE BROWSER, e.g.
# ws://192.168.0.104:7880 on a LAN, or wss://voice.example.com behind a TLS
# reverse proxy. The server hands it to clients together with the token.
# Unset = clients fall back to the build-time NEXT_PUBLIC_LIVEKIT_URL.
#LIVEKIT_URL=ws://localhost:7880
#
# --- https deployments need wss:// ---
# A browser refuses to open a ws:// connection from an https:// page (mixed
# content), and livekit-server cannot terminate TLS itself — its config parser
# rejects a `tls:` block (verified against v1.13.7). So an https deployment
# must put *something* in front of the SFU:
#
#  1. The bundled Caddy proxy: add `voice-tls` to COMPOSE_PROFILES below. It
#     reuses the certificate the app already serves (./certs is mounted into
#     the proxy at the same path as the app, so TLS_CERT_FILE/TLS_KEY_FILE
#     apply unchanged); with autocert (TLS_DOMAINS) set LIVEKIT_TLS_CERT_FILE
#     / LIVEKIT_TLS_KEY_FILE to the cached /app/certs/<domain>/<domain>.crt
#     and .key instead. Open LIVEKIT_PROXY_PORT and point LIVEKIT_URL at
#     wss://<your domain>:<LIVEKIT_PROXY_PORT>.
#  2. Or, if the host already runs a reverse proxy (nginx / Caddy /
#     Cloudflare) in front of the stack, proxy the SFU there instead — LiveKit
#     needs a plain WebSocket passthrough, no special headers:
#         location /livekit/ {
#             proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:7880/;
#             proxy_http_version 1.1;
#             proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
#             proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
#             proxy_set_header Host $host;
#         }
#     then LIVEKIT_URL=wss://<your domain>/livekit
#
# Both ways also need the UDP media range reachable from the browser (see
# LIVEKIT_UDP_MIN_PORT below) — TLS covers signalling only.
# Host port of the bundled Caddy proxy (profile `voice-tls`).
#LIVEKIT_PROXY_PORT=7443
# Lifetime of the minted access tokens (hours).
#LIVEKIT_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=6
# UDP media range livekit-server relays audio on. Open it in the firewall
# (UDP), plus 7880/tcp and 7881/tcp.
#LIVEKIT_UDP_MIN_PORT=50000
#LIVEKIT_UDP_MAX_PORT=50100
# Advertise the host's real address in ICE candidates. true is right for the
# compose setup (host networking): on a LAN it resolves to the interface
# address, on a cloud VM to the public one. The TCP fallback on 7881 covers
# networks that block UDP entirely.
#LIVEKIT_USE_EXTERNAL_IP=true
#TURN_EXTERNAL_IP=
# Frontend build-time override of the whole ICE list (JSON RTCIceServer
# array), e.g. to point at an external TURN provider instead of coturn.
#NEXT_PUBLIC_ICE_SERVERS='[{"urls":"turn:turn.example.com:3478","username":"user","credential":"pass"}]'
